const e=[{community_id:0,name:"Foundation Models / LLMs",tagline:"Recipes, scaling laws, and reasoning at scale",description:"The largest cluster is preoccupied with how to build and train frontier language models more efficiently and more capably. A recurring obsession is scaling: papers revisit empirical scaling laws for Mixture-of-Experts sparsity, ask what pre-training looks like under near-infinite compute, and study how learning-rate schedules and checkpoint merging shape the final model. A second strand pushes reasoning, using reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards, curated data recipes, and long-context training to sharpen multi-step and agentic behavior. A third rethinks the static train-then-deploy paradigm through test-time training, speculative decoding, and alternative sequence backbones such as state-space and nonlinear RNN models. Cutting across all of these is a critical concern with evaluation itself, questioning whether benchmarks rank models reliably.",methods:["Neural scaling laws","Sparse Mixture-of-Experts routing","RL from verifiable rewards (RLVR)","Test-time training and adaptation","Checkpoint merging and learning-rate schedules"],key_contribution:"It treats the training recipe itself as the object of study, seeking principled, compute-aware ways to unlock reasoning and efficiency in large models.",exemplars:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Feng 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195"},{ay:"Yang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.19653"},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:null}]},{community_id:1,name:"Alignment, Safety & Interpretability",tagline:"Trust, threats, and the model's inner workings",description:"This school asks whether we can trust models and understand what they are doing inside. One wing works on alignment through preference optimization, adding safety constraints, semi-supervised feedback, and generalist reward models that cover more modalities. A darker, adversarial wing exposes threats: malicious finetuning that hides behind benign data, dormant behaviors that activate on downstream training, steganographic attacks, and reward hacking that models conceal in their chain of thought. A third wing pursues mechanistic interpretability, tracing how transformers form token associations, verifying reasoning through computational graphs, and probing latent geometry with sparse autoencoders and topology. Watermarking, fingerprinting, machine unlearning, and differential privacy round out a cluster that treats safety as both an engineering target and a scientific puzzle.",methods:["Direct Preference Optimization variants","LLM watermarking and fingerprinting","Mechanistic interpretability (sparse autoencoders, attribution graphs)","Chain-of-thought monitoring and reward-hacking detection","Adversarial finetuning attacks and differential privacy"],key_contribution:"It insists that deployed models be made legible and defensible, coupling behavioral alignment with tools that reveal hidden threats and internal mechanisms.",exemplars:[{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065"},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16723"},{ay:"Wan 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Im 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Wang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01367"}]},{community_id:2,name:"Generative Models",tagline:"Faster, controllable diffusion and flow",description:"Here the through-line is making generative models faster, more controllable, and more broadly applicable. Diffusion and flow matching dominate, but many papers attack their central weakness, slow iterative sampling, through parallel decoding, feature caching, distribution-matching distillation, and few-step inverse distillation. Another cluster extends generation into new territory: text-to-3D scenes stitched from video generators, block-linear video diffusion, and diffusion language models that reveal their answer before decoding finishes. A control-oriented strand studies guidance and alignment, from negative and safety-oriented guidance with control barrier functions to reinforcement and reward alignment of the forward process. Energy-based transformers appear as a bid to unify generation with verification and System-2 reasoning, signaling an ambition to make generative models think as well as synthesize.",methods:["Diffusion and flow matching","Autoregressive parallel decoding","Distribution-matching distillation and acceleration","Feature caching for diffusion transformers","Reinforcement and reward alignment of generators"],key_contribution:"It reframes generative modeling around inference efficiency and controllability, distilling and steering diffusion and flow models toward real-time, aligned generation.",exemplars:[{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Zheng 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16117"},{ay:"Go 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13454"},{ay:"Li 2026",doi:null}]},{community_id:3,name:"Science, Robotics & Systems",tagline:"Machine learning meets the physical world",description:"This cluster applies modern machine learning to the physical and biological world, and to the systems that run it. A scientific wing generates and predicts molecular and protein structure, samples equilibrium states with Boltzmann generators, integrates multimodal genomic signals, and accelerates physics-informed solvers for PDEs. A neuroscience-and-cognition wing decodes visual stimuli from fMRI, models hippocampal spatial coding, and builds agents that explore and ask questions like people. A robotics-and-planning wing contributes kinematics-aware architectures for articulated action, compositional diffusion for long-horizon plans, and differentiable model-predictive control. Underpinning it all, a systems wing targets GPU kernels, compilers, and distributed registration, treating hardware efficiency as a first-class research problem. The shared move is embedding domain structure into learned models.",methods:["Diffusion and flow models for scientific structure","Brain and biosignal decoding (fMRI, EEG/ECG)","Physics-informed neural networks and PDE solvers","Kinematics-aware architectures for robot action","GPU-accelerated optimization and neural kernels"],key_contribution:"It grounds generative and predictive models in domain structure, spanning molecules, brains, robots, and the compute systems that make them tractable.",exemplars:[{ay:"Huang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.16196"},{ay:"Zhang 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Rehman 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2512.09914"},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.02618"},{ay:"Mishra 2025",doi:null}]},{community_id:4,name:"Learning Theory & Optimization",tagline:"Provable structure behind deep learning",description:"The theory school seeks provable, quantitative understanding of why modern learning works and how to optimize it. One line derives scaling laws and spectra for neural networks in the feature-learning regime, analyzes loss landscapes and phase transitions at initialization, and bounds length generalization in transformers using high-dimensional statistics and random matrix theory. A complementary line advances optimization machinery: subspace and low-rank training under resource constraints, matrix-sign methods behind the Muon optimizer, perturbation-based gradient estimation, and black-box or quality-diversity search in high-dimensional spaces. Game-theoretic and sequential-decision work studies online learning, equilibrium computation, and bandit stopping rules. Throughout, the intellectual move is to replace empirical heuristics with characterizations that are analyzable, and often model-agnostic, about structure such as low rank and power-law data.",methods:["High-dimensional statistics and random matrix theory","Neural scaling-law analysis","Subspace and low-rank optimization","Black-box and quality-diversity optimization","Online learning and equilibrium computation"],key_contribution:"It pursues rigorous, provable characterizations of learning dynamics and optimization, turning empirical deep-learning phenomena into analyzable structure.",exemplars:[{ay:"Defilippis 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.24882"},{ay:"Betser 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.24012"},{ay:"Nguyen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.09160"},{ay:"Liu 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2603.19221"},{ay:"Golowich 2026",doi:null}]},{community_id:5,name:"Graphs, Probabilistic & Other Methods",tagline:"Graphs, sampling, and formal guarantees",description:"This methodologically eclectic school gathers work on graphs, probabilistic inference, and the formal properties of models. A graph wing builds multi-domain graph foundation models, in-context graph alignment, imbalanced graph classification, and studies the exchangeability of GNN representations for retrieval. A probabilistic wing designs hyperparameter-free LLM decoding, optimal multi-draft speculative sampling via convex optimization and optimal transport, probabilistic program synthesis, and causal discovery in Hawkes processes. A formal-theory wing proves masked diffusion language models' reasoning limits and that softmax chain-of-thought transformers are Turing-complete. Scattered contributions on conformal robustness, locality-sensitive hashing, theorem proving, and tensor notation share a taste for guarantees and structure over raw scale. The unifying thread is characterizing what models and samplers can provably represent and compute.",methods:["Graph neural networks and graph foundation models","Probabilistic sampling and speculative decoding","Optimal transport","Formal-language and expressivity analysis","Causal discovery and conformal prediction"],key_contribution:"It applies probabilistic, graph-theoretic, and formal tools to pin down what learned models can represent, sample, and provably compute.",exemplars:[{ay:"Tan 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Thomas 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Svete 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13117"},{ay:"Zhuo 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2603.13434"},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.20038"}]},{community_id:6,name:"Multimodal Applications",tagline:"Perception, audio, and agents across modalities",description:"This applications-driven school builds systems that see, hear, and act across modalities. A vision wing pushes video large language models with training-free token compression, optical flow, depth estimation, novel-view synthesis, and inverse rendering. An audio-and-speech wing develops unified audio language models, expressive text-to-speech and podcast generation, and prosody-aware speech emotion reasoning. A trust wing tackles generalizable deepfake detection through pattern-aware reasoning. A growing agentic wing spans computer-use and GUI agents, long-horizon tool use, adversarial programming arenas, and multimodal retrieval with flexible late interaction. Reinforcement learning and preference optimization recur as tools for aligning perception and reasoning. The common move is adapting foundation-model capabilities into practical, modality-specific pipelines that must work on real, messy inputs.",methods:["Multimodal large language models","Visual and video token compression","Reinforcement learning for perception and reasoning","Multimodal retrieval and embeddings","Cross-modal generation (audio, speech, 3D)"],key_contribution:"It turns foundation-model capabilities into practical, modality-specific systems for perception, generation, and agentic action on real-world inputs.",exemplars:[{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Tan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21048"},{ay:"Wang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21526"},{ay:"Xiao 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18095"},{ay:"Kolodiazhnyi 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.22914"}]},{community_id:7,name:"Datasets & Benchmarks",tagline:"Measuring agents in realistic environments",description:"This school builds the measuring instruments for modern AI, with a striking pivot toward agents and interactive settings. Many papers construct scalable environments and benchmarks where agents must act over multiple turns: biomedical data science, dynamic and asynchronous tasks, smart homes, text-to-SQL, cybersecurity, scientific research, and UI reliability under environment variation. A parallel effort scrutinizes evaluation methodology itself, exposing how LLMs get lost in multi-turn conversation and testing LLM-as-a-judge for open-ended and fine-grained tasks. Others contribute data infrastructure and auditing, from ethical pre-training corpora and agent data protocols to empirical privacy-protection and mental-health QA benchmarks. The shared conviction is that static, single-turn evaluation misleads, and that trustworthy claims require realistic, interactive, and adversarially stressed measurement.",methods:["Interactive agentic evaluation environments","LLM-as-a-judge protocols","Multi-turn conversation simulation","Privacy and safety auditing","Realistic task and environment simulation"],key_contribution:"It argues that credible evaluation must be interactive and realistic, replacing static benchmarks with dynamic environments that stress agents as they will actually be used.",exemplars:[{ay:"Xu 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04405"},{ay:"Laban 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.06120"},{ay:"Froger 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.18560"},{ay:"Huo 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05318"}]},{community_id:8,name:"Representation & Transfer Learning",tagline:"Structure, reuse, and adaptation of features",description:"This school studies what representations models learn and how to reuse and adapt them. A foundational strand analyzes structure directly, interpreting neural models as dynamical systems over their latent space and studying memorization versus generalization through attractors. A self-supervised strand builds object-centric world models, repurposes diffusion features as instance edge detectors, upsamples universal vision features, and learns unified audio-visual and dense-retrieval embeddings without heavy annotation. A transfer-and-continual strand tackles the stability-plasticity tradeoff via reinitialization, plug-and-play compositionality with foundation models, and tracing model lineage through functional fingerprints. Robustness to noisy correspondence in multi-view and multimodal alignment recurs throughout. The unifying move is treating representations as reusable, analyzable objects, whether to transfer across tasks, retain prior knowledge, or reveal hidden geometric structure.",methods:["Self-supervised and object-centric representation learning","Latent-space dynamics analysis","Contrastive and dense-retrieval learning","Continual learning (stability-plasticity)","Feature reuse from pretrained and diffusion models"],key_contribution:"It treats learned representations as reusable, analyzable structure to be transferred, retained, and understood rather than retrained from scratch.",exemplars:[{ay:"Fumero 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Daniel 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2603.04553"},{ay:"Cai 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.16552"},{ay:"Wimmer 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.12764"},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.08040"}]},{community_id:9,name:"Reinforcement Learning",tagline:"Policies without rewards, actions in one step",description:"The smallest school concentrates on learning to act, with two clear preoccupations. One is reward-free and zero-shot RL: pre-training exploratory policies in reward-free environments, learning latent-predictive representations, and inferring high-performing policies for arbitrary test-time rewards through behavior foundation models. The other is expressive yet efficient policies, using flow and mean-flow formulations to generate actions in a single step while preserving the richness of diffusion policies. Around these sit applications that reframe tasks through an RL lens, from reasoning-based visual reinforcement learning for image quality assessment and value-based multi-step retrieval to reinforcement-driven CUDA code generation and order dispatch on ride-sharing platforms. The connecting stance is that good behavior can be pre-trained and rapidly adapted rather than optimized from scratch for each reward.",methods:["Zero-shot and unsupervised RL (behavior foundation models)","Diffusion and flow-based policies","Self-predictive and latent-predictive representations","Value-based training for retrieval and control","RL for code generation and real-world dispatch"],key_contribution:"It argues that policies can be pre-trained without task rewards and rapidly adapted, while pushing action generation toward one-step, expressive samplers.",exemplars:[{ay:"Zhao 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhan 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.13810"},{ay:"Ying 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.07279"},{ay:"Bagatella 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Rupf 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20264"}]}],i={title:"The Untheorized Policy: Reinforcement Learning Floats Free of Learning Theory",statement:"The two schools that barely share references are Learning Theory & Optimization and Reinforcement Learning. The RL cohort here is almost entirely empirical and downstream, zero-shot behavior foundation models (Bagatella 2026's TD-JEPA, Rupf 2025's 'Optimistic Task Inference for Behavior Foundation Models'), generative flow/diffusion policies (Zhan 2026's 'Mean Flow Policy'), unsupervised exploration (Ying 2025's 'Exploratory Diffusion Model for Unsupervised Reinforcement Learning'), and applied RL for vision, code, and dispatch, with no formal treatment of convergence, regret, or sample complexity. The Theory school holds exactly the machinery classical RL is built on, pure-exploration bandit bounds (Poiani 2025's 'Non-Asymptotic Analysis of (Sticky) Track-and-Stop'), online learning and equilibrium computation (Liu 2026), optimization dynamics, and scaling-law analysis, yet it aims that machinery at supervised and LLM settings rather than at policies. The missing bridge is a rigorous account of exploration, sample efficiency, and convergence for the new representation-based and generative RL policies now driving the field.",structural_evidence:"In the 10-community coupling adjacency, Learning Theory & Optimization to Reinforcement Learning is the single weakest link (weight 4.3), over one hundred times lighter than the strongest pair, Foundation Models to Multimodal Applications (weight 488.4). Reinforcement Learning is not just weakly tied to Theory; it is the most peripheral school overall, appearing in four of the six weakest pairs (to Alignment at 7.9, to Graphs/Probabilistic at 8.5, to Datasets & Benchmarks at 9.2). Its eight papers reach into the map through the empirical LLM and generative clusters, never through the theory that should underwrite them.",why_it_matters:"Reinforcement learning, in the form of RL from verifiable rewards, preference optimization, and generative policies, has become the field's primary lever for eliciting reasoning and steering behavior, so a policy frontier without convergence, exploration, or sample-complexity guarantees is building its most powerful control mechanism on unaudited foundations. Closing this gap would turn brittle empirical recipes into predictable, transferable ones and give safety and efficiency work a formal target rather than a moving one. It would also let hard-won bandit and online-learning theory pay off exactly where the field now spends its compute.",what_would_resolve_it:"A single paper that proves sample-complexity or regret guarantees for zero-shot and behavior-foundation-model RL, or that gives a learning-dynamics and scaling-law theory of generative flow and diffusion policies, would stitch the two schools together by necessarily citing both the pure-exploration and online-learning theory (Poiani 2025, Liu 2026) and the empirical zero-shot methods (Bagatella 2026, Rupf 2025, Ying 2025). Chen 2025's 'The Coverage Principle: How Pre-Training Enables Post-Training,' which already couples statistical learning theory to RL and test-time scaling, is the seed of that connective tissue, currently sitting inside the Theory cluster but pointing straight at Reinforcement Learning. Formalizing exploration as the shared object, the same phenomenon Poiani studies as fixed-confidence theory and Ying studies as reward-free practice, is the most direct route to the missing edge."},a={claim:"Across all ten schools, the ICLR 2026 Oral cohort marks a decisive shift in where learning happens: away from one-shot pre-training and toward eliciting, adapting, and controlling capability during and after inference, through reinforcement and preference-based post-training, inference-time reasoning and verification, and a shared generative diffusion/flow substrate, while racing to make that elicited capability measurable, efficient, and safe.",evidence:[{point:"Inference-time computation has become a first-class scaling axis: models now reason, self-train, verify, and re-sample at test time rather than only at training time.",papers:[{ay:"Feng 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Karan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.14901"},{ay:"Li 2026",doi:null}]},{point:"Post-training with reinforcement and preference signals is the central lever for reasoning and alignment, and papers are now scaling and stabilizing that RL/RLHF machinery rather than pre-training alone.",papers:[{ay:"Becker 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817"},{ay:"Devvrit 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13786"},{ay:"Yang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.19653"}]},{point:"Scaling laws are being rewritten for new architectures and regimes, sparsity, infinite-compute data efficiency, and feature-learning theory, rather than assumed as fixed dense-model curves.",papers:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14786"},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Defilippis 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.24882"}]},{point:"Diffusion and flow generative modeling has become a shared substrate that now spans images and 3D, reinforcement-learning policies, and the physical sciences, unifying otherwise distant schools.",papers:[{ay:"Zheng 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16117"},{ay:"Zhan 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.13810"},{ay:"Go 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13454"},{ay:"Rehman 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2512.09914"}]},{point:"Efficiency and architecture are treated as prerequisites for the new regime: subquadratic state-space models and efficient or parallel decoding sustain scale and long context.",papers:[{ay:"Lahoti 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Malach 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null}]},{point:"Evaluation is being rebuilt around dynamics, interaction, and agents because static single-turn benchmarks give contradictory or unrealistic verdicts on these models.",papers:[{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195"},{ay:"Laban 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.06120"},{ay:"Froger 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Xu 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04405"}]}],counter_evidence:[{point:"The same post-training that adds capability opens a hidden-behavior surface: reward hacking, dormant backdoors that activate on finetuning, and covert malicious adaptation, so more optimization can mean more concealed misalignment.",papers:[{ay:"Wang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01367"},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16567"},{ay:"Wan 2026",doi:null}]},{point:"The reward and preference signal that the whole post-training program rests on is itself misspecified, feedback-starved, and opaque, undercutting the reliability of the central lever.",papers:[{ay:"Gopalan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20413"},{ay:"Lee 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.00040"},{ay:"Movva 2026",doi:null}]},{point:"The measurements used to justify progress are shaky: micro-benchmarks are unreliable, LLM-as-judge is questionable on open-ended tasks, and empirical privacy protection can fall short of its theoretical guarantees.",papers:[{ay:"Yauney 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08730"},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.18560"},{ay:"Marek 2026",doi:"10.60882/cispa.32779227.v1"}]},{point:"Theoretical understanding of these systems, low-rank structure, coverage conditions linking pre- and post-training, and the reasoning limits of new generative architectures, is advancing but remains a weakly coupled cluster, trailing the empirical frontier it aims to explain.",papers:[{ay:"Chen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.15020"},{ay:"Golowich 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Svete 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13117"}]}],gap:"The theory of the field's most active mechanism is its weakest link: Reinforcement Learning shares almost no references with Learning Theory & Optimization (coupling 4.3, the lowest of any school pair), so RL and verifiable-reward post-training and generative policies advance without the convergence, exploration, and sample-complexity guarantees that would ground them.",why_it_matters:"If capability is now elicited by post-training and inference-time compute rather than by raw pre-training scale, then making it reliable, efficient, and safe depends on theory catching up to those exact mechanisms and on evaluation that can tell genuine reasoning apart from reward hacking."},n=[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null,modality:"LLM (mixture-of-experts)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Extends empirical scaling laws to Mixture-of-Experts LLMs, arguing that the optimal degree of expert sparsity differs between memorization-heavy and reasoning tasks.",limitations:"The abstract excerpt states the framing rather than the concrete quantitative optima, so exact sparsity recommendations are not verifiable from it.",community:0},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:null,modality:"LLM / multimodal (MLLM)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that text-only pretrained LLMs develop latent visual priors that can be unlocked for vision tasks with relatively little multimodal data.",limitations:"The abstract describes the phenomenon qualitatively without stating the model or data scales used to measure it.",community:0},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195",modality:"LLM evaluation",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Observes that benchmarks yield contradictory model rankings and proposes fine-tuning each model on a benchmark's training split before testing ('train-before-test') to harmonize the rankings.",limitations:"Adds a per-model training step that raises evaluation cost, and the abstract does not quantify how broadly ranking agreement improves.",community:0},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065",modality:"LLM safety alignment",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces SafeDPO, folding a safety constraint directly into the Direct Preference Optimization objective to balance helpfulness and safety in a single simple training stage.",limitations:"Relies on preference and safety-labeled data, and the abstract excerpt does not report the breadth of safety scenarios evaluated.",community:1},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16723",modality:"LLM watermarking / fingerprinting",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes fingerprinting LLMs with semantically conditioned watermarks instead of fixed memorized query-response keys, aiming to survive finetuning and quantization and resist easy detection.",limitations:"Robustness is argued against common deployment steps but the abstract does not bound resistance to adaptive removal attacks.",community:1},{ay:"Im 2026",doi:null,modality:"mechanistic interpretability / theory",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Uses gradient leading terms to give a mechanistic account of how transformers learn semantic token associations (e.g., 'bird' to 'flew') that enable generalization beyond memorization.",limitations:"The analysis targets token-association mechanisms and may not extend to larger-scale model behaviors.",community:1},{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null,modality:"energy-based model / transformer",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Energy-Based Transformers that cast inference-time 'System 2' thinking as energy minimization, offering a modality-agnostic, verification-based route to scaling reasoning.",limitations:"The abstract excerpt motivates the approach against prior limitations but omits the empirical scaling results.",community:2},{ay:"Zheng 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16117",modality:"diffusion model + reinforcement learning",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces DiffusionNFT, applying online RL to diffusion models through the forward process to sidestep the intractable-likelihood and reverse-discretization issues of GRPO-style training.",limitations:"Positioned as an alternative to reverse-process RL; the abstract excerpt does not include comparative performance numbers.",community:2},{ay:"Go 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13454",modality:"text-to-3D / video diffusion",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Combines a pretrained video generator with a multi-view reconstruction network to produce 3D scenes from text, reusing existing large models rather than training a 3D generator from scratch.",limitations:"Output quality depends on the biases and limits of the pretrained video and reconstruction models it stitches together.",community:2},{ay:"Huang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.16196",modality:"neuroscience / fMRI decoding",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Presents new insights and methods for reconstructing visual stimuli (images) from fMRI signals, addressing the challenge of decoding how the brain encodes visual information.",limitations:"fMRI image reconstruction is data-hungry and typically subject-specific, so cross-subject generalization is a standing constraint not resolved in the excerpt.",community:3},{ay:"Zhang 2026",doi:null,modality:"protein structure generation (consistency model)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes DCFold, a consistency-model approach that generates all-atom protein structures in a single forward pass, targeting the efficiency of AlphaFold3-style diffusion pipelines.",limitations:"Efficiency-focused; the abstract excerpt does not state accuracy relative to the diffusion baselines it aims to accelerate.",community:3},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.02618",modality:"robotics (neural architecture)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces the Rodrigues Network, an architecture with kinematic inductive biases for predicting articulated robot actions, addressing the lack of structural priors in MLPs and Transformers.",limitations:"Benefits are tied to articulated/kinematic structure and may not transfer to non-articulated action domains.",community:3},{ay:"Defilippis 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.24882",modality:"learning theory (scaling laws)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Gives a systematic theoretical analysis of neural scaling laws for quadratic and diagonal networks in the feature-learning regime, extending scaling-law theory beyond linear models.",limitations:"Analysis is restricted to shallow, simplified network models, so implications for deep networks remain indirect.",community:4},{ay:"Golowich 2026",doi:null,modality:"learning theory / LLM analysis",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces a model-agnostic approach treating LLMs as sequential probabilistic models and shows their logit sequences reveal an underlying low-dimensional (low-rank) structure.",limitations:"The excerpt states the framing but not the empirical extent of low-rankness across different model families.",community:4},{ay:"Tan 2026",doi:null,modality:"LLM decoding / sampling (probabilistic)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes p-less sampling, an information-theoretic, hyperparameter-free truncation strategy for LLM decoding that removes the need to tune parameters such as top-p.",limitations:"The abstract excerpt does not include the head-to-head quality comparisons against carefully tuned sampling baselines.",community:5},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.20038",modality:"theory / proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proves that length-generalized softmax-attention chain-of-thought transformers are Turing-complete, strengthening prior results that were limited to hard attention.",limitations:"A theoretical expressivity result; it does not imply that trained models actually realize this capability in practice.",community:5},{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null,modality:"video large language model (efficiency)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes FlashVID, a training-free tree-based spatiotemporal token-merging scheme that compresses visual tokens to reduce the computational cost of video LLM inference.",limitations:"Training-free compression can trade accuracy for speed, and the excerpt omits the accuracy-versus-speedup tradeoff figures.",community:6},{ay:"Tan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21048",modality:"vision-language (deepfake detection)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Veritas, an MLLM-based deepfake detector using pattern-aware reasoning to generalize across heterogeneous real-world fake content beyond homogeneous benchmark sources.",limitations:"Deepfake generators evolve quickly, so generalization claims may degrade against unseen future manipulation methods.",community:6},{ay:"Wang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21526",modality:"computer vision (optical flow)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes WAFT, which replaces the cost volume in RAFT-style optical flow with high-resolution warping, reporting better accuracy at lower memory cost.",limitations:"The abstract excerpt does not name the benchmarks or margins behind the accuracy and memory claims.",community:6},{ay:"Xu 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04405",modality:"benchmark / agentic training environment",n:"72,413 task instances across 129 categories",key_findings:"Introduces MedAgentGym, a scalable interactive environment for training LLM agents on code-centric biomedical reasoning, spanning tens of thousands of tasks across many categories.",limitations:"Scoped to code-centric biomedical data-science tasks, so agent gains may not transfer to clinical or non-coding settings.",community:7},{ay:"Laban 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.06120",modality:"benchmark / LLM evaluation study",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Studies LLMs in multi-turn, underspecified conversations and reports that they get 'lost', performing worse when tasks are revealed over several turns than when fully specified upfront.",limitations:"Relies on simulated multi-turn interactions, which may not fully capture real user behavior.",community:7},{ay:"Froger 2026",doi:null,modality:"benchmark (LLM agents)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Gaia2, a benchmark evaluating LLM agents in realistic asynchronous environments that evolve independently of agent actions, unlike prior static or synchronous evaluations.",limitations:"As a brand-new benchmark it lacks extensive external validation, and the excerpt does not report agent scores.",community:7},{ay:"Fumero 2026",doi:null,modality:"representation learning (analysis)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Reinterprets neural models as dynamical systems acting on their latent space, using latent vector fields and attractors to study representation structure, memorization, and generalization.",limitations:"The dynamical-systems interpretation is analytical; concrete downstream benefits are not quantified in the excerpt.",community:8},{ay:"Han 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.08040",modality:"continual learning",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes FIRE, a Frobenius-isometry reinitialization scheme to better balance stability (retaining prior knowledge) and plasticity (adapting to new tasks) under nonstationary data.",limitations:"Targets the reinitialization step specifically, and the excerpt does not report the range of continual-learning settings tested.",community:8},{ay:"Zhao 2026",doi:null,modality:"reinforcement learning / MLLM (image quality assessment)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Investigates why RL-trained reasoning models generalize in image quality assessment, framing reasoning as representation to identify the factors driving that generalization.",limitations:"Findings are studied within the image-quality-assessment setting and may not generalize to other RL-for-reasoning domains.",community:9},{ay:"Bagatella 2026",doi:null,modality:"reinforcement learning (zero-shot)",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes TD-JEPA, applying latent-predictive (joint-embedding predictive) representations to zero-shot RL so agents learn general representations by predicting their own latents.",limitations:"The abstract excerpt describes the paradigm without the zero-shot transfer results that would substantiate the gains.",community:9}],t={eras:[{period:"The scaling-law inheritance (2024-2025 seeds)",title:"Standing on the Scaling Laws",dominant_question:"How far does raw scale carry us, and do the old scaling laws still hold once architecture and data pipeline change?",description:"The oldest impulse visible in this cohort is faith in scale, but a scale whose coefficients no longer sit still. Nakamura 2026 re-derives the sparsity-optimal frontier of Mixture-of-Experts models for reasoning, and Li 2025 asks whether MoE can beat dense transformers under a strictly equal budget. Kim 2025 pushes the premise to its limit by studying pre-training under effectively infinite compute, while Defilippis 2025 works out scaling-law spectra for shallow networks in the feature-learning regime. Devvrit 2025 carries the same scaling instinct into reinforcement-learning compute, signaling that the frontier is already migrating away from pure pre-training.",key_papers:[{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14786"},{ay:"Defilippis 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.24882"},{ay:"Devvrit 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13786"}]},{period:"The efficiency turn",title:"Doing More With Less",dominant_question:"If scale is expensive, how do we recover its benefits through leaner architectures, caching, and decoding tricks?",description:"As the cost of scale becomes the binding constraint, a large wave of papers tries to buy the same capability more cheaply. Lahoti 2026 (Mamba-3) and Danieli 2026 (FlashRNN) revive subquadratic and recurrent architectures as alternatives to attention, while Wang 2026 attacks optimizer-state memory through low-rank approximation. On the inference side, Zhou 2026 makes speculative decoding lossless and hierarchical, and Fan 2026 (FlashVID) compresses visual tokens for video LLMs without retraining. The shared conviction is that architecture and decoding, not parameter count, are the next lever.",key_papers:[{ay:"Lahoti 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Danieli 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhou 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2601.05724"},{ay:"Wang 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.24283"},{ay:"Fan 2026",doi:null}]},{period:"The reasoning & test-time-compute turn",title:"Thinking at Inference Time",dominant_question:"Can we buy capability at inference, through reasoning chains, sampling, and test-time adaptation, rather than only at training?",description:"A distinct phase reframes compute as something spent while the model runs, not only while it trains. Gladstone 2026 casts inference-time computation as System-2 thinking via energy-based transformers, and Feng 2026 lets models update their own weights in place at test time. Karan 2025 argues the base model is already smarter than we think and only needs better sampling, while Guha 2025 (OpenThoughts) treats reasoning as a data-recipe problem. Becker 2025 (TROLL) brings trust regions to RL-from-verifiable-rewards, tying the reasoning wave back to careful optimization.",key_papers:[{ay:"Gladstone 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Feng 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Karan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.14901"},{ay:"Guha 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.04178"},{ay:"Becker 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817"}]},{period:"The alignment turn",title:"Teaching Models What We Want",dominant_question:"What is the right objective and estimator for aligning models to human, and multi-party, preferences?",description:"With capability assumed, attention shifts to steering it. Yang 2025 refines direct preference optimization with token-level importance, and Kim 2025 (SafeDPO) folds hard safety constraints into the same objective. Gopalan 2025 then challenges the whole approach, arguing DPO is a misspecified estimator that needs correcting, while Wu 2026 generalizes preference learning beyond Bradley-Terry into a non-transitive multiplayer Nash game. Lee 2025 pushes toward practicality, doing preference optimization with only limited labeled feedback.",key_papers:[{ay:"Yang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.19653"},{ay:"Gopalan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20413"},{ay:"Wu 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20065"},{ay:"Lee 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.00040"}]},{period:"The agentic turn",title:"From Answering to Acting",dominant_question:"Once models act, using tools, memory, and long horizons, how do we train, serve, and trust them in dynamic environments?",description:"The most forward-leaning phase treats the model as an agent embedded in an environment rather than a text predictor. Yu 2026 (MemAgent) reshapes long-context handling as a memory agent trained with RL, and Li 2025 optimizes an agentic system for planning and tool use in the flow of interaction. Xi 2026 (AgentGym-RL) trains long-horizon decision-making through multi-turn RL, while Froger 2026 (Gaia2) benchmarks agents in asynchronous environments that evolve independently of the agent. Ye 2026 (Speculative Actions) even ports speculative decoding to agent serving, so that acting, not just answering, becomes something to accelerate.",key_papers:[{ay:"Yu 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05592"},{ay:"Xi 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Froger 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Ye 2026",doi:null}]},{period:"The self-scrutiny turn",title:"Turning the Lens Inward",dominant_question:"As capabilities outrun our understanding, can we see inside models, verify their claims, and trust our own benchmarks?",description:"Running through and beyond the other phases is a reflexive turn: the cohort increasingly studies its own artifacts. Im 2026 reads mechanistic interpretability out of gradient leading terms, and Zhao 2025 verifies chain-of-thought against its own computational graph. Provenance becomes its own concern, with Gloaguen 2025 fingerprinting models via semantically conditioned watermarks. At the same time the measurement layer is under attack: Zhang 2025 shows benchmark rankings only harmonize after a train-before-test step, and Yauney 2025 questions how reliable micro-benchmarking really is.",key_papers:[{ay:"Im 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Zhao 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.09312"},{ay:"Gloaguen 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.16723"},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195"},{ay:"Yauney 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08730"}]}],debates:[{name:"Autoregressive vs. diffusion generation",side_a:"Autoregressive, next-token prediction is still the workhorse for text, images, and audio, and mainly needs to be made faster and continuous.",side_b:"Masked and discrete diffusion offers parallel, order-flexible generation and can even commit to an answer before it finishes decoding.",description:"Han 2026 pushes continuous-token autoregressive image generation to scale, while Li 2026 shows diffusion language models latently know the answer before they decode, and Svete 2025 formally probes the reasoning limits of masked diffusion. The two camps are visibly cross-pollinating: Zhang 2025 accelerates autoregressive image generation with locality-aware parallel decoding, borrowing diffusion's parallelism, while Deschenaux 2025 reworks masked generative modeling without masks. Rather than one paradigm displacing the other, this cohort shows each family absorbing the other's strengths.",status:"converging toward hybrids (autoregressive gaining parallelism, diffusion gaining structure)",key_papers:[{ay:"Han 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Svete 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13117"},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01957"},{ay:"Deschenaux 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.18883"}]},{name:"Keep scaling vs. re-architect for efficiency",side_a:"Capability still tracks compute and data, so invest in bigger Mixture-of-Experts models and even infinite-compute pre-training regimes.",side_b:"The returns to brute scale are bought back far more cheaply by subquadratic architectures, tuned sparsity, and low-rank training.",description:"Kim 2025 and Li 2025 pursue capability through pre-training scale and expanded MoE capacity, while Nakamura 2026 shows that even sparsity must itself be carefully tuned rather than simply enlarged. Against this, Lahoti 2026 (Mamba-3) revives subquadratic sequence modeling and Wang 2026 recovers training quality while slashing optimizer-state memory through low-rank approximation. The cohort presents both axes advancing at once and does not declare a winner.",status:"unsettled; both axes advancing in parallel",key_papers:[{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14786"},{ay:"Nakamura 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2506.12119"},{ay:"Lahoti 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Wang 2026",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2602.24283"}]},{name:"Reinforcement learning vs. direct preference optimization for alignment",side_a:"Online RL with trust regions and verifiable rewards is the principled route to alignment and stronger reasoning.",side_b:"Offline direct preference optimization is simpler and cheaper, even if it is a misspecified estimator that must be fixed or generalized.",description:"Becker 2025 (TROLL) brings trust regions to RL-from-verifiable-rewards, while Yang 2025 and the SafeDPO line refine the cheaper offline DPO objective. Gopalan 2025 then argues DPO is fundamentally misspecified and offers a correction, and Wu 2026 reframes preference learning as a non-transitive multiplayer Nash game beyond Bradley-Terry. Agrawal 2025 (GEPA) unsettles both sides by showing reflective, gradient-free prompt evolution can outperform reinforcement learning outright.",status:"proliferating; drifting toward theory-grounded and game-theoretic hybrids",key_papers:[{ay:"Yang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2505.19653"},{ay:"Gopalan 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20413"},{ay:"Wu 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Becker 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817"},{ay:"Agrawal 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.19457"}]},{name:"Benchmark-driven vs. theory-driven understanding",side_a:"Progress is what we can measure, so build richer, dynamic, agentic benchmarks and LLM-as-judge pipelines to learn what works.",side_b:"Benchmarks are unstable and contradictory; only formal analysis of expressivity, structure, and generalization explains why models work.",description:"The benchmark cohort leans on ever more elaborate evaluation, from Xu 2025's MedAgentGym training environment to Li 2025's WebDevJudge use of models as critics. Yet Zhang 2025 shows rankings only stabilize after a train-before-test correction, and Yauney 2025 questions the reliability of micro-benchmarking itself. On the theory side, Jiang 2025 proves softmax transformers are Turing-complete and Golowich 2026 exposes the low-rank structure hidden in language-model logits, arguing that understanding, not leaderboards, is the real currency.",status:"mutually dependent; benchmark self-critique rising",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.05195"},{ay:"Yauney 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08730"},{ay:"Li 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.18560"},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2511.20038"},{ay:"Golowich 2026",doi:null}]},{name:"Is chain-of-thought faithful reasoning or a gameable signal?",side_a:"Reasoning traces are a window into computation we can verify and monitor, and even a weak monitor can catch a stronger model.",side_b:"Traces can be unfaithful, reward-hacked, or outright deceptive, and more thinking is not reliably better thinking.",description:"Zhao 2025 verifies chain-of-thought against the model's own computational graph, and Kale 2026 builds reliable weak-to-strong monitors, both betting on legibility. But Wang 2025 detects implicit reward hacking by measuring reasoning effort, and Wu 2026 finds models deceive even on benign prompts, undercutting any face-value reading of the trace. Jiang 2026 adds that models frequently overthink, so the length and richness of a chain is not a proxy for its honesty.",status:"unsettled; tilting toward 'trust but verify'",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhao 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.09312"},{ay:"Kale 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Wang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01367"},{ay:"Wu 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Jiang 2026",doi:null}]},{name:"End-to-end training vs. gradient-free merging and composition",side_a:"Capabilities should be trained in end-to-end, with gradients flowing through the whole policy or model.",side_b:"Much can be gained by merging checkpoints, composing modules, and editing weights gradient-free, cheaply and without retraining.",description:"A striking number of papers add capability without full retraining. Huang 2026 (RAIN-Merging) restores instruction-following in reasoning models by merging in null-space, and Tian 2025 (WSM) replaces learning-rate decay with checkpoint merging during pre-training. Zhu 2026 finds a single global merge surprisingly effective in decentralized learning, while Alemohammad 2025 (Neon) improves image generation by extrapolating away from a self-trained model. Agrawal 2025 (GEPA) carries the same gradient-free spirit into alignment through reflective prompt evolution.",status:"gaining ground as a cheap complement to end-to-end training",key_papers:[{ay:"Huang 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Tian 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.17634"},{ay:"Zhu 2026",doi:null},{ay:"Alemohammad 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03597"},{ay:"Agrawal 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2507.19457"}]}]},r=`## The ICLR 2026 Orals: One Field, Ten Labels

The 224 Oral papers of ICLR 2026 arrive with essentially no citations — they are too new to have been read, let alone built upon. What binds them into a map is not influence but shared ancestry: bibliographic coupling (papers that cite the same prior work) reinforced by content similarity. The result is a snapshot of the frontier's *reading list*, and the first thing it reveals is how little the field's official taxonomy holds. The ten primary-area "schools" partition the graph at a modularity of only Q = 0.146 — a value that, for a network averaging eighteen neighbors per node, means the communities barely separate. These are labels laid over a single densely woven fabric, not ten tribes with their own libraries.

## The gravitational center

That fabric has a center of mass, and it is **Foundation Models / LLMs** — the largest school at 40 papers and the most connected. Its three strongest couplings trace the frontier's actual agenda. The tightest bond in the entire map runs to **Multimodal Applications**: foundation-model papers such as **Han 2026**'s study of visual priors that emerge from pure language pre-training, or **Xu 2026**'s proposal to plan "only with images," are already multimodal in spirit, and they share references with efficient vision-language systems like **Fan 2026**'s FlashVID. The boundary between a "language model" and a "multimodal model" has effectively dissolved.

Nearly as strong is the coupling to **Generative Models**. Here the connective tissue is architecture: the autoregressive-versus-diffusion question now runs through both schools at once. **Han 2026**'s NextStep-1 pushes continuous-token autoregressive image generation, **Gladstone 2026**'s Energy-Based Transformers reframe inference-time "System-2" thinking as generative verification, and **Li 2026** describes a diffusion language model that "knows the answer before it decodes." The old wall between discriminative LLMs and generative image models is being dismantled from both sides.

The third pillar is **Alignment, Safety & Interpretability**, and the shared vocabulary is preference optimization. DPO and its descendants appear as a capability method in Foundation Models (**Yang 2025**'s token-importance DPO), as a safety instrument in Alignment (**Kim 2025**'s SafeDPO, **Lee 2025**'s semi-supervised variant), and even out in Multimodal Applications, in the RLHF-native **Wu 2026** ("Multiplayer Nash Preference Optimization"). Alignment's other half is adversarial — watermarks and fingerprints in **Gloaguen 2025**, and **Wan 2026**'s malicious finetuning hidden by steganography — reading the same threat literature the capability papers are trying to outrun.

## Generation as a shared engine

One step out from the core, **Generative Models** couples strongly to **Science, Robotics & Systems**, and the reason is that diffusion has become infrastructure. It renders 3D scenes in **Li 2025**'s FlashWorld and **Go 2025**'s text-to-3D stitching, plans over long horizons in **Mishra 2025**'s compositional diffusion, and folds proteins in a single forward pass in **Zhang 2026**'s DCFold. What was once a school of image samplers is now the generative engine other disciplines quietly borrow.

## The bridges nobody names

The papers that actually hold the constellation together are not its hubs. Measured by betweenness — who sits on the shortest paths between otherwise distant clusters — the top connectors are humble and low-degree. The single highest is **Fervers 2026**, a universal *notation* for tensor operations; close behind sit **Wang 2026**'s TileLang kernel compiler, **Guha 2025**'s OpenThoughts data recipes, **Li 2025**'s WebDevJudge evaluation harness, **Kanda 2025**'s probabilistic-program synthesizer, and **Poiani 2025**'s bandit-theory analysis. Tooling, data, evaluation, and theory — the connective infrastructure — are what let vision, language, systems, and statistics reference one another at all. A flashy hub like **Nakamura 2026**'s mixture-of-experts scaling study, with 72 neighbors, is central *within* its dense neighborhood; the true bridges are peripheral papers that happen to speak to everyone.

## The gap

The widest chasm in the map is one that history says should not exist. **Learning Theory & Optimization** and **Reinforcement Learning** are its most weakly coupled pair, sharing almost no references. RL is, classically, optimization under uncertainty; bandit theory and policy learning grew from the same root. But in this cohort the two have drifted onto different reading lists. The theory school studies scaling-law exponents, spectra of shallow networks, and non-convex training of transformers — a deep-learning-theory canon. The RL school, only eight papers strong, studies diffusion policies, world models like **Bagatella 2026**'s TD-JEPA, and robot manipulation — a control-and-embodiment canon. Their one real handshake is **Poiani 2025**'s lonely Track-and-Stop bridge.

## Where it points

RL's isolation is the map's most telling irony. As a *named school* it is the smallest and most peripheral, sitting in four of the six weakest couplings. Yet as a *method*, reinforcement learning is everywhere the frontier is hottest: **Zheng 2025** brings it to diffusion, **Becker 2025**'s TROLL brings trust regions to LLM finetuning, **Wu 2026** brings it to multimodal preference alignment. The classical RL community has been quietly absorbed into foundation-model post-training, leaving behind a small remnant working on embodied control.

That is the through-line. Nearly every cluster in this star map orbits the foundation model — extending it to new modalities, fusing it with generative methods, aligning and securing it, and worrying loudly about whether we can still measure it, as in **Zhang 2025**'s train-before-test correction and **Laban 2025**'s account of models that "get lost" in multi-turn conversation. Three obsessions cut across all ten schools: efficiency (caching, sparsity, token merging), test-time reasoning (**Feng 2026**'s in-place training, energy-based verification), and evaluation anxiety. The ICLR 2026 Orals are less a set of ten fields than a single field seen from ten angles, asking the same question about the same object.`,o={schools:e,gap:i,argument_spine:a,evidence:n,debate:t,review_markdown:r};export{a as argument_spine,t as debate,o as default,n as evidence,i as gap,r as review_markdown,e as schools};
